### 6.6 Invariant Mass Shift

Unlike the cross section which drops rapidly, the di-jet \(A_{LL}\) is expected to be a slowly varying function of the invariant mass. Because of this, the full unfolding procedure used in the cross section analysis was not used in the extraction of the asymmetry. The determination of the particle or parton level mass of each data point was made by applying a simple mass shift to each point.

The mass shifts were found by comparing the detector level di-jet masses to the masses of matching particle or parton level di-jets in simulation. The procedure for finding particle and parton level di-jets which match to detector level di-jets is described in section 5.2.1. The mass shift for a given bin was calculated by finding the difference between the particle or parton level di-jet mass and the detector level di-jet mass on an event-by-event basis for all di-jets which fall into that given mass bin. Once this difference has been found for each event, the mass shift in a bin is just the mean value of the distribution. Figure 6.7

