Placing the proper beam polarization, relative luminosity, and yield factors into the above equation (and using \(\delta\) to signify the uncertainty), the expression for the statistical error on \(A_{LL}\) presented in section 6 is recovered:

\[\delta A_{LL}=\frac{\left(\sum_{i}P_{Yi}^{2}P_{Bi}^{2}\left[\left(N_{i}^{++}+N _{i}^{--}\right)+R_{3i}^{2}\left(N_{i}^{+-}+N_{i}^{-+}\right)\right]\right)^{ \frac{1}{2}}}{\sum_{i}P_{Yi}^{2}P_{Bi}^{2}\left[\left(N_{i}^{++}+N_{i}^{--} \right)+R_{3i}\left(N_{i}^{+-}+N_{i}^{-+}\right)\right]}\] (D.15)As a final exercise, a very simple expression for the statistical error can be found by assuming the beam polarizations are constant and that the relative luminosity is unity. With these assumptions, it is easy to see that the statistical uncertainty can be approximated as:

\[\delta A_{LL}=\frac{1}{P_{Y}P_{B}}\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\] (D.16)

where \(P_{Y}\) and \(P_{B}\) are the average yellow and blue beam polarizations, respectively, and \(N\) is the total di-jet yield. Large discrepancies between this approximation and the errors given by equation D.15 are an indication of a mistake in the calculation of the statistical uncertainties.

